Upstream CVE in Crypt::Age, reported by CPANSec on 2026-09-04, publication
planned for 2026-09-18. The fix is tracked in p5-crypt-age as k45.

UPSTREAM PROBLEM
Crypt::Age did not bound the number of X25519 stanzas in an age header. Every
stanza costs a scalar multiplication on decrypt before anything is
authenticated -- the header MAC key is derived from the file key, which does
not exist until a stanza has been unwrapped. Measured on Crypt-Age 0.003: 4000
stanzas (382 KB) = 15.7 s of CPU, then rejection. Linear, ~3.9 ms per stanza.

WHY IT IS WORSE HERE
File/SOPS/Backend/Age.pm loops over @$age_keys in decrypt_data_key and passes
each entry separately to Crypt::Age->decrypt. A SOPS document carries any
number of age entries, so the cost is entries x stanzas x identities rather
than just stanzas x identities -- whoever may hand us a SOPS document
multiplies both factors themselves. Nothing on the path bounds the size first.

TO DO once the upstream fix is out
1. Raise the cpanfile pin from Crypt::Age 0.003 to 0.004, which carries the cap.
2. Decide whether the age backend additionally bounds the number of age entries
   per document. The upstream cap applies per blob, not per document -- the
   outer loop stays unbounded otherwise.
3. Crypt::Age gained a max_stanzas parameter (default 128). Decide whether the
   age backend should pass it through.

No edits from p5-crypt-age; this ticket is the handover.

